WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
JENNY BROWN: Today, we're
talking about a new Cloud

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.630
region, open-source security
protocol, and a Cloud Logging

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.530
update.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.530
I'm Jenny Brown, and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.890
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.810
Digital tools like
cloud computing

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.320
are fueling economic
transformation across the US.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.800
For example, Google's investment
in cities and communities

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.910
across Ohio is bringing
over 200 jobs to the state

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.940
and is helping provide
almost $13 billion

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.050
of economic activity for tens
of thousands of Ohio businesses.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.390
And to accelerate
that transformation,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.240
we are thrilled to announce
the newest Google Cloud region

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.350
in Columbus.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.830
The Columbus Cloud region is
open and brings a second region

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.980
to the Midwest, the 10th
region in North America,

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.910
and grows our global
Cloud region count to 33.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.490
We believe that PSP
can provide a number

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.430
of significant security
benefits for the industry.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.770
Given its proven track record
in our production environment,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.880
we hope that it can become a
standard for scalable, secure

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.610
communication across a
wide range of settings

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.170
and applications.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.860
To support this, we are
making PSP open source

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.710
to encourage broader
adoption by the community.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.780
Check out the blog
for more info.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.080
We are thrilled to announce
the release of a new update

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.990
to the Cloud Logging
library for Node.js

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.140
with the key new features
of improved error handling

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.200
and writing structured logging
to standard output, which

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.190
is handy if you run applications
in serverless environments,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.960
like Google Functions.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.170
Head to the blog post for
a full list of details.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.480
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.370
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.140
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.140
Leave us a comment or tweet us,
and we'll see you in the Cloud.

